WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Smart pointers are most useful when they communicate ownership intent.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.440
Use std::unique_ptr when one owner is responsible for an object.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:17.660
It is the clearest default for heap ownership because transfer must be explicit, and accidental copying is prevented.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.640
A unique owner makes the lifetime easier to trace and usually easier to test.

5
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.160
Use std::shared_ptr only when ownership is genuinely shared.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:35.340
Shared ownership can be appropriate for objects whose lifetime is naturally controlled by multiple independent consumers, but it also makes destruction less obvious.

7
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:42.080
Overusing shared ownership can hide design problems and make it difficult to know when an object should go away.

8
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:51.540
If shared objects refer back to each other, consider std::weak_ptr for non-owning observation so the graph does not keep itself alive unexpectedly.

9
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.140
Smart pointers do not automatically repair poor lifetime design.

10
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:05.040
A raw pointer or reference can still be the right tool for short-lived, non-owning access, especially in function parameters where the caller retains responsibility.

11
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:13.220
The important distinction is that non-owning access must not be stored or extended beyond the owner's lifetime unless a clear contract protects it.

12
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.360
During review, ask whether each pointer-like type answers the ownership question.

13
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.380
Who owns this object?

14
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.520
Who may observe it?

15
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.900
Who may transfer it?

16
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.280
When those answers are visible in the interface, memory safety becomes a design property instead of a guessing exercise.

17
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:38.640
Prefer factory functions that return ownership directly, and avoid APIs that require callers to mix allocation policy with business logic.

18
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.420
When shared ownership appears, ask why one owner is not enough and how cycles are prevented.

19
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.640
When raw access appears, ask whether the lifetime is shorter than the call and whether null is an expected state.

20
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:57.740
These questions keep smart pointers connected to design intent instead of turning them into mechanical replacements.
